     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Tue, Feb 10, 2015 at 8:11 PM, tanya longabach <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mizz...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi Phil,</div><div> </div><div>I am trying to figure out how the df for X_2 statistic is calculated. I read the articles that you referenced in the itemfit section, and they give examples of several methods in which df can be calculated (e.g., some take into account the intervals in which the responders are divided, others take into consideration the number of parameters estimated etc. ) I assume that the X_2 calculated in mirt for item fit takes into consideration the number of groups the responders are divided into, because there is an option to specify approximately how many subjects are in each group, correct?</div></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>Correct, but that being said I really think you shouldn&#39;t use the X^2 statistic unless you have an extremely large sample size and a good number of items. It just doesn&#39;t behave well since it requires multiple estimation stages and binning.</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
